const items = [["t55_ag_good", () => "The dog kind of love is real love. Some people also know a love that's a whole different size, like God's, or whatever opened up for them in prayer or on a long retreat, and I don't want to lump that in with the dog. If you've been there, don't shrink it down to a thought experiment about a friend.\n\nIf you haven\u2019t been there yet (and even if you have), I have good news for you!", "c7daf4ae525620e92a52dc8440985d8ee0a250d41b6a7583bc5f43bad089d2ef"], ["t55_ag_g1", () => "One practical door anyone can use to slowly experience divine love is gratitude (even after the Turkey\u2019s done & Thanksgiving is over!). And gratitude isn\u2019t just one way to experience divine love for the first time, or to get it back when the feeling fades. I think it\u2019s essential if we want to live in that love instead of occasionally visiting it.", "735e6d31f3c3d1051524710b837055a7724a872043594412af1e5377eef452ef"], ["t55_ag_g2", () => "This is a bit tricky to put into words, but let me try anyway\u2026 You may be suffering now, but you could be suffering more. That\u2019s something to be grateful for. I\u2019m not talking just about changing your \u201cglass half empty\u201d perception to a glass \u201chalf full perception.\u201d I\u2019m not pushing \u201ctoxic positivity.\u201d And I\u2019m certainly not preaching \u201cprosperity philosophy,\" even though it can seem to start at that. I'm ultimately reaching for something much deeper.", "cd26f7b50df33f1647dba3577ece3115dd77a075ff7f528276872652499b89a1"], ["t55_ag_g3", () => "If you have a moment right now to breathe, that\u2019s something to be grateful for. I learned this many times the hard way. For example, when I got tuberculosis from a cave in Mexico at age 20, and coughed for six months straight, eventually literally unable to breathe, leading to a near death experience. And then in Senegal, I had a suspected bilharzia infection in 2021, and could only painfully pee blood, I promised to always be thankful if I could ever pee normally again. Miraculously, I then found some obscure ethnobotanical research (on [Google Scholar](http://scholar.google.com), before AI) which led me to drink roselle tea. Within an hour, I was cured. I don\u2019t always remember to say \u201cthank you\u201d when I pee now, but I try.", "23568789ba0415996d933a43dcacebe52a15841c808cae255fdd765313cb203b"], ["t55_ag_g4", () => "That may sound like a silly, quirky waste of time to some folks. But what I notice is that saying thank you for every little thing, whenever I remember to, is not about imposing some kind of \u201cspiritual discipline.\u201d It is directly reconnecting me to something pure and sacred. And even (or especially) when I am suffering, I try to remember to be grateful for that also, because if not for suffering, I would not even think about how much I have enjoyed not suffering as much, and how much it will help me to develop more compassion, wisdom, and gratitude in future. It connects me with the plight of the countless other beings suffering at that very moment, authentically bringing up a wellspring of compassion in my heart for all of us. And I see clearly that it is my inner child who is feeling this pain, and what an honor it is that I can be there for him.", "eb71cab16d8b47cb8cb87a994a2695594e7462229c1503b98f49d135b28ef7f0"], ["t55_unt_p3", () => "These can all be real gifts. The bargain begins when your worth depends on them, or when you feel you have to keep performing them to be loved. Your little one can hang onto those bargains as if belonging were a matter of life or death, especially if you haven't learned to parent him or her yourself yet. Even with good parents, you could have learned this when people repeatedly praised what you did rather than your inherent worth. That wasn't your flaw. Self-differentiation is learning to notice when you're making that trade, and to keep your own ground instead.", "277ffd09e2b6dfc8ae8de670bd51db8dc415596e45db39966e4213ce14758cc1"], ["t55_heal_p", () => "Don't be afraid to ask your therapist/yourself \"What is this practice supposed to help, and how would I know if it's helping?\" This sovereign, gently curious stance should apply to everything in life. Feeling calmer or having deeper insights or explanations for the trauma response are some possible signs of initial progress, but if that's the endgame, it's just a cope. Real healing is ultimately measured by reduced need for the therapy. It's like real parenting: you might start out reading parenting books and thinking hard about what to say or do, but after a while you get into the groove and a lot of it becomes natural. Inner parenting can be like that too. You don't need to keep checking this guide or planning every conversation with your little one; the nurturing, protecting and guiding become more automatic. You might still turn to therapy when you need it, and life will keep offering chances to grow wiser and more compassionate. And if the therapy is actually working, it should improve your daily life, and especially the health of the inner child-inner adult relationship. Going at it harder isn't as useful as going at it with more wisdom and compassion. Take the parts that work, and innovate on the rest.", "c76c364619dc3c8aebda0bfc0490d7f8a4bff835607a41fb2b65cb9be2d97afc"], ["t55_boc_p1", () => "A complete ideal parent may be impossible to imagine (unless you saw The Brady Bunch). And imagination is pretty hard for adults anyway since it kinda gets stamped out of kids at an early age, but it's not just a foolish waste of time. It's actually a genius mode unlock. So since the adult is actually a baby when it comes to power to imagine, start small: Borrow one function at a time from a figure who embodies it. Perhaps a therapist or your grandmother really cared for you. Perhaps your teacher at some age was a great guide. Maybe an older sibling protected you well. And there's always your future healed self if you can imagine that.", "7e5aa10847e47e481614297945bf8010bc19621cc61bfc42d376ea6275163bfd"], ["t55_boc_p3", () => "Most of us wouldn't open up to somebody who'd only just stopped being mean, or let them take care of us. That takes warmth, and if there isn't any yet, borrow some. Think of someone you remember fondly. How would they sit beside your little one? Just make sure it's someone who liked you the way you were.", "89b502312beb950e6e831614fc6e8e92785e705e958d067d7f31177911f81a12"], ["t55_ag_h3", () => "### Ask God for a Loan\n\nThe dog kind of love is real love. Some people also know a love that's a whole different size, like God's, or whatever opened up for them in prayer or on a long retreat, and I don't want to lump that in with the dog. If you've been there, don't shrink it down to a thought experiment about a friend.\n\nIf you haven\u2019t been there yet (and even if you have), I have good news for you!\n\n#### Your Loan is PRE-APPROVED!\n\nOne practical door anyone can use to slowly experience divine love is gratitude (even after the Turkey\u2019s done & Thanksgiving is over!). And gratitude isn\u2019t just one way to experience divine love for the first time, or to get it back when the feeling fades. I think it\u2019s essential if we want to live in that love instead of occasionally visiting it.\n\nThis is a bit tricky to put into words, but let me try anyway\u2026 You may be suffering now, but you could be suffering more. That\u2019s something to be grateful for. I\u2019m not talking just about changing your \u201cglass half empty\u201d perception to a glass \u201chalf full perception.\u201d I\u2019m not pushing \u201ctoxic positivity.\u201d And I\u2019m certainly not preaching \u201cprosperity philosophy,\" even though it can seem to start at that. I'm ultimately reaching for something much deeper.\n\nIf you have a moment right now to breathe, that\u2019s something to be grateful for. I learned this many times the hard way. For example, when I got tuberculosis from a cave in Mexico at age 20, and coughed for six months straight, eventually literally unable to breathe, leading to a near death experience. And then in Senegal, I had a suspected bilharzia infection in 2021, and could only painfully pee blood, I promised to always be thankful if I could ever pee normally again. Miraculously, I then found some obscure ethnobotanical research (on [Google Scholar](http://scholar.google.com), before AI) which led me to drink roselle tea. Within an hour, I was cured. I don\u2019t always remember to say \u201cthank you\u201d when I pee now, but I try.\n\nThat may sound like a silly, quirky waste of time to some folks. But what I notice is that saying thank you for every little thing, whenever I remember to, is not about imposing some kind of \u201cspiritual discipline.\u201d It is directly reconnecting me to something pure and sacred. And even (or especially) when I am suffering, I try to remember to be grateful for that also, because if not for suffering, I would not even think about how much I have enjoyed not suffering as much, and how much it will help me to develop more compassion, wisdom, and gratitude in future. It connects me with the plight of the countless other beings suffering at that very moment, authentically bringing up a wellspring of compassion in my heart for all of us. And I see clearly that it is my inner child who is feeling this pain, and what an honor it is that I can be there for him.\n\nOf course, that\u2019s not to say you should not try to suffer less. The point of life, according to every spiritual tradition and religion, I think, is to suffer less, which first requires us to understand the cause of suffering, and what real suffering and real happiness are. If you, like Biggie Smalls, have come to understand that having money is not real happiness, that\u2019s a big deal! That\u2019s something to be grateful for.\n\nNotice how, when you offer thanks (to the universe, to life, to God, whatever you may believe in), a space in your heart opens up. It might not feel immediately like you suddenly know the real meaning of Divine Love, but it\u2019s a little spark of \u2026 something.... There\u2019s a tiny bit of freedom, of peace, stillness, softness. And as that softness deepens each time, you start to understand what I mean by Divine Love. Many people have written books on this topic (like [A. Helwa\u2019s book](https://authorahelwa.com/), Secrets of Divine Love, for example), so I won\u2019t belabor the point further here, but I hope you are able to experience this when you try it out for a while, and I\u2019d really love to know how it works for you!\n\nOnce you start to feel that lovely softness of the heart, invite your little one in with you as your spiritual integration practice. They might go numb, or call BS on that. If so, they might just need to see if you're actually humble enough, and grateful enough for them, to listen to their rejection and be patient. If you instead retreat back into your cozy corner, it's the food bank feast all over again: You can have an enormous spiritual feast upstairs while the child is still starving downstairs.", "eaa3faf5e07354e237659ff7a5814c0e60e11f89faf5f1ca9d50e20c5efffcd9"], ["t55_borrow_h2", () => "## Borrow Love\u2014or Borrow the Perspective of Care\n\nSometimes love isn't the missing part. Your dog can chew up your good shoes and be forgiven before you've found the other one. Try giving yourself a fraction of that and it goes flat, or starts to feel weirdly unsafe. So let the dog go first. Or your kid, or whoever you love without trying; they don't have to be in the room. Just feel that for a minute, in your body, before you try to turn it into self-love. Then see if some of it can go to your little one, who hasn't even chewed anything.\n\nWatch how it lands, though. \"I care about you\" can be patient, or it can be \"I care about you, now hurry up and feel better.\" Getting close can be too much, too, the way kids squirm away from an aunt they barely know who goes in for the big hug, and then climb all over the uncle who's just sitting there. And if nothing moves at all, don't push it. Knowing that love is in you somewhere counts for a lot, and you're borrowing something you already know how to do.\n\nOr put somebody you care about into your exact life, your family, your bank account and all, and then step outside it and ask what you'd tell them. It's a [cheap trick for becoming the outsider to your own mess](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/21728454/). The quest for self-knowledge is a life's journey, but you don't have to wait for your life to finish to figure out your best next move. And the gap it gets around even has a name, [Solomon's paradox](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/24916084/), after the king everybody brought their disputes to, who then wrecked his own life.\n\nThen take a small piece of whatever you'd tell them and hand it to yourself. If the advice was \"get more sleep,\" then for now just try brushing your teeth before midnight instead of at two. That's how you learn as a kid, and it works now, too. The technical term for this is \"[scaffolding](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Instructional_scaffolding).\"\n\nIf the only time the Nurturer shows up is when something's wrong, your little one can start to feel like they're your job. Sometimes show up just to say \"I'm glad you're here,\" and then enjoy them for a minute, the way you'd enjoy a little kid who wants to show you a rock they found.\n\n### A Smaller Doorway: Goodwill\n\nSometimes \"I love you\" is more than you can honestly say, especially to somebody who's treated you badly. Even the usual whittled-down loving-kindness prayer, \"May you be happy,\" can feel like rewarding them. That's why I go with Buddha's overall wish (paraphrasing): **\"May you be loving, peaceful, and free,\"** since if they were all of those, they wouldn't have done what they did, and then they actually would be lovable.\n\nThis goodwill isn't just for the target of it, though. You may find it bounces back and opens up a little warmth in your heart. And if \"I love you\" goes flat when you aim it at yourself or your little one, the same wish works there, too. \ud83e\udef6\ud83c\udfff\n\n### Ask God for a Loan\n\nThe dog kind of love is real love. Some people also know a love that's a whole different size, like God's, or whatever opened up for them in prayer or on a long retreat, and I don't want to lump that in with the dog. If you've been there, don't shrink it down to a thought experiment about a friend.\n\nIf you haven\u2019t been there yet (and even if you have), I have good news for you!\n\n#### Your Loan is PRE-APPROVED!\n\nOne practical door anyone can use to slowly experience divine love is gratitude (even after the Turkey\u2019s done & Thanksgiving is over!). And gratitude isn\u2019t just one way to experience divine love for the first time, or to get it back when the feeling fades. I think it\u2019s essential if we want to live in that love instead of occasionally visiting it.\n\nThis is a bit tricky to put into words, but let me try anyway\u2026 You may be suffering now, but you could be suffering more. That\u2019s something to be grateful for. I\u2019m not talking just about changing your \u201cglass half empty\u201d perception to a glass \u201chalf full perception.\u201d I\u2019m not pushing \u201ctoxic positivity.\u201d And I\u2019m certainly not preaching \u201cprosperity philosophy,\" even though it can seem to start at that. I'm ultimately reaching for something much deeper.\n\nIf you have a moment right now to breathe, that\u2019s something to be grateful for. I learned this many times the hard way. For example, when I got tuberculosis from a cave in Mexico at age 20, and coughed for six months straight, eventually literally unable to breathe, leading to a near death experience. And then in Senegal, I had a suspected bilharzia infection in 2021, and could only painfully pee blood, I promised to always be thankful if I could ever pee normally again. Miraculously, I then found some obscure ethnobotanical research (on [Google Scholar](http://scholar.google.com), before AI) which led me to drink roselle tea. Within an hour, I was cured. I don\u2019t always remember to say \u201cthank you\u201d when I pee now, but I try.\n\nThat may sound like a silly, quirky waste of time to some folks. But what I notice is that saying thank you for every little thing, whenever I remember to, is not about imposing some kind of \u201cspiritual discipline.\u201d It is directly reconnecting me to something pure and sacred. And even (or especially) when I am suffering, I try to remember to be grateful for that also, because if not for suffering, I would not even think about how much I have enjoyed not suffering as much, and how much it will help me to develop more compassion, wisdom, and gratitude in future. It connects me with the plight of the countless other beings suffering at that very moment, authentically bringing up a wellspring of compassion in my heart for all of us. And I see clearly that it is my inner child who is feeling this pain, and what an honor it is that I can be there for him.\n\nOf course, that\u2019s not to say you should not try to suffer less. The point of life, according to every spiritual tradition and religion, I think, is to suffer less, which first requires us to understand the cause of suffering, and what real suffering and real happiness are. If you, like Biggie Smalls, have come to understand that having money is not real happiness, that\u2019s a big deal! That\u2019s something to be grateful for.\n\nNotice how, when you offer thanks (to the universe, to life, to God, whatever you may believe in), a space in your heart opens up. It might not feel immediately like you suddenly know the real meaning of Divine Love, but it\u2019s a little spark of \u2026 something.... There\u2019s a tiny bit of freedom, of peace, stillness, softness. And as that softness deepens each time, you start to understand what I mean by Divine Love. Many people have written books on this topic (like [A. Helwa\u2019s book](https://authorahelwa.com/), Secrets of Divine Love, for example), so I won\u2019t belabor the point further here, but I hope you are able to experience this when you try it out for a while, and I\u2019d really love to know how it works for you!\n\nOnce you start to feel that lovely softness of the heart, invite your little one in with you as your spiritual integration practice. They might go numb, or call BS on that. If so, they might just need to see if you're actually humble enough, and grateful enough for them, to listen to their rejection and be patient. If you instead retreat back into your cozy corner, it's the food bank feast all over again: You can have an enormous spiritual feast upstairs while the child is still starving downstairs.\n\n### When the Spiritual Relationship Hurts\n\nIf a spiritual teacher you trusted betrayed you, even sitting down to meditate can feel like it belongs to them now. Kids quit piano over one bad teacher, and this is way worse. But if you let an abuser control your appreciation of life, then you've given them an unearned win.\n\nBeing the grown-up doesn't mean controlling everything yourself, though. After a betrayal like that, part of you might want to. Spiritual support can help you act where it's yours to act, and let go of outcomes and other people's choices where it isn't. The [Serenity Prayer](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Serenity_Prayer) is the example I'd give, if prayer still feels like yours.", "e02e997b2c0e21a3e9f3d8cdfb2c97e446f710b43d93c07f843723970f9a2d86"], ["t55_boc_h2", () => "## Borrow One Competency at a Time\n\nA complete ideal parent may be impossible to imagine (unless you saw The Brady Bunch). And imagination is pretty hard for adults anyway since it kinda gets stamped out of kids at an early age, but it's not just a foolish waste of time. It's actually a genius mode unlock. So since the adult is actually a baby when it comes to power to imagine, start small: Borrow one function at a time from a figure who embodies it. Perhaps a therapist or your grandmother really cared for you. Perhaps your teacher at some age was a great guide. Maybe an older sibling protected you well. And there's always your future healed self if you can imagine that.\n\nIf the love still isn't coming, you can at least stop judging your little one. Obviously no insults. Helpful advice is great, we all need advice. Let them absorb it at their pace. And don't expect a thank-you for the session, like they booked it. That's enough to start being the adult, even if it isn't enough yet for them to trust you.\n\nMost of us wouldn't open up to somebody who'd only just stopped being mean, or let them take care of us. That takes warmth, and if there isn't any yet, borrow some. Think of someone you remember fondly. How would they sit beside your little one? Just make sure it's someone who liked you the way you were.\n\nFor protecting, ask what a minimally competent adult would do here. Maybe something simple, like actually smelling that old food you're about to eat and then trashing it if it smells a little weird. If you're about to send an angry text, hit pause. If the room itself triggers your spidey sense, leave, or lock the door and get help. Even if this still feels like the child acting like the adult, for now, that's ok. \"Fake it til you make it\" isn't exactly it, but yeah. Afterward, you can tell your little one, \"I did that for us. That was the Protector.\" If it sounds funny, laugh!\n\nThe Guide can be you too, just a calmer you. That's a lot easier to imagine than your future healed self. Think back to what (and how) you decided about something like this the last time you were calm. Or ask what the wise person you respect would have you practice today. A wise tradition (like \"What would Jesus do?\" or \"What would Buddha do?\") can give some direction, too, until you develop your own internal compass.\n\nThe thank-you you weren't waiting for might not come anyway. \ud83d\ude1c More like \"Oh, so NOW you care?\" Which, fair enough, you did take a while. Your first instinct might be \"But I'm trying!\", and then it's just two six-year-olds arguing. Some days, just hearing it out like a decent adult is the only thing you'll manage to borrow, and your little one might still roll their eyes at you. \ud83d\ude44", "87b788cc1880ba47b30c6c3a963d74e93e4e9cfd227f204016463fb632fae159"], ["t55_borrow_h1", () => "# Borrow the Adult Before You Can Be the Adult\n\n## Noticing Counts\n\n\"Huh, somebody in here is really scared.\" That might be the most adult thing you've got right now, and it's plenty to start with. Whoever's noticing isn't all the way inside the scared one.\n\nIt can help to use your own name. For me, instead of \"I'm terrified,\" that'd be \"Little Joel is terrified. What does Little Joel need right now?\" You'll sound a bit like Elmo :) In [lab studies](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5495792/), though, people who talked to themselves this way reacted less to upsetting pictures and memories, and their brains didn't seem to be working any harder to do it.\n\nIf you can tell your parts apart pretty easily, you've probably got plenty of noticing already. Don't keep drilling it just because the love isn't coming yet. A lot of meditators are great at watching. I was, and baby me was still starving.\n\n## Borrow One Competency at a Time\n\nA complete ideal parent may be impossible to imagine (unless you saw The Brady Bunch). And imagination is pretty hard for adults anyway since it kinda gets stamped out of kids at an early age, but it's not just a foolish waste of time. It's actually a genius mode unlock. So since the adult is actually a baby when it comes to power to imagine, start small: Borrow one function at a time from a figure who embodies it. Perhaps a therapist or your grandmother really cared for you. Perhaps your teacher at some age was a great guide. Maybe an older sibling protected you well. And there's always your future healed self if you can imagine that.\n\nIf the love still isn't coming, you can at least stop judging your little one. Obviously no insults. Helpful advice is great, we all need advice. Let them absorb it at their pace. And don't expect a thank-you for the session, like they booked it. That's enough to start being the adult, even if it isn't enough yet for them to trust you.\n\nMost of us wouldn't open up to somebody who'd only just stopped being mean, or let them take care of us. That takes warmth, and if there isn't any yet, borrow some. Think of someone you remember fondly. How would they sit beside your little one? Just make sure it's someone who liked you the way you were.\n\nFor protecting, ask what a minimally competent adult would do here. Maybe something simple, like actually smelling that old food you're about to eat and then trashing it if it smells a little weird. If you're about to send an angry text, hit pause. If the room itself triggers your spidey sense, leave, or lock the door and get help. Even if this still feels like the child acting like the adult, for now, that's ok. \"Fake it til you make it\" isn't exactly it, but yeah. Afterward, you can tell your little one, \"I did that for us. That was the Protector.\" If it sounds funny, laugh!\n\nThe Guide can be you too, just a calmer you. That's a lot easier to imagine than your future healed self. Think back to what (and how) you decided about something like this the last time you were calm. Or ask what the wise person you respect would have you practice today. A wise tradition (like \"What would Jesus do?\" or \"What would Buddha do?\") can give some direction, too, until you develop your own internal compass.\n\nThe thank-you you weren't waiting for might not come anyway. \ud83d\ude1c More like \"Oh, so NOW you care?\" Which, fair enough, you did take a while. Your first instinct might be \"But I'm trying!\", and then it's just two six-year-olds arguing. Some days, just hearing it out like a decent adult is the only thing you'll manage to borrow, and your little one might still roll their eyes at you. \ud83d\ude44\n\n## Borrow Love\u2014or Borrow the Perspective of Care\n\nSometimes love isn't the missing part. Your dog can chew up your good shoes and be forgiven before you've found the other one. Try giving yourself a fraction of that and it goes flat, or starts to feel weirdly unsafe. So let the dog go first. Or your kid, or whoever you love without trying; they don't have to be in the room. Just feel that for a minute, in your body, before you try to turn it into self-love. Then see if some of it can go to your little one, who hasn't even chewed anything.\n\nWatch how it lands, though. \"I care about you\" can be patient, or it can be \"I care about you, now hurry up and feel better.\" Getting close can be too much, too, the way kids squirm away from an aunt they barely know who goes in for the big hug, and then climb all over the uncle who's just sitting there. And if nothing moves at all, don't push it. Knowing that love is in you somewhere counts for a lot, and you're borrowing something you already know how to do.\n\nOr put somebody you care about into your exact life, your family, your bank account and all, and then step outside it and ask what you'd tell them. It's a [cheap trick for becoming the outsider to your own mess](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/21728454/). The quest for self-knowledge is a life's journey, but you don't have to wait for your life to finish to figure out your best next move. And the gap it gets around even has a name, [Solomon's paradox](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/24916084/), after the king everybody brought their disputes to, who then wrecked his own life.\n\nThen take a small piece of whatever you'd tell them and hand it to yourself. If the advice was \"get more sleep,\" then for now just try brushing your teeth before midnight instead of at two. That's how you learn as a kid, and it works now, too. The technical term for this is \"[scaffolding](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Instructional_scaffolding).\"\n\nIf the only time the Nurturer shows up is when something's wrong, your little one can start to feel like they're your job. Sometimes show up just to say \"I'm glad you're here,\" and then enjoy them for a minute, the way you'd enjoy a little kid who wants to show you a rock they found.\n\n### A Smaller Doorway: Goodwill\n\nSometimes \"I love you\" is more than you can honestly say, especially to somebody who's treated you badly. Even the usual whittled-down loving-kindness prayer, \"May you be happy,\" can feel like rewarding them. That's why I go with Buddha's overall wish (paraphrasing): **\"May you be loving, peaceful, and free,\"** since if they were all of those, they wouldn't have done what they did, and then they actually would be lovable.\n\nThis goodwill isn't just for the target of it, though. You may find it bounces back and opens up a little warmth in your heart. And if \"I love you\" goes flat when you aim it at yourself or your little one, the same wish works there, too. \ud83e\udef6\ud83c\udfff\n\n### Ask God for a Loan\n\nThe dog kind of love is real love. Some people also know a love that's a whole different size, like God's, or whatever opened up for them in prayer or on a long retreat, and I don't want to lump that in with the dog. If you've been there, don't shrink it down to a thought experiment about a friend.\n\nIf you haven\u2019t been there yet (and even if you have), I have good news for you!\n\n#### Your Loan is PRE-APPROVED!\n\nOne practical door anyone can use to slowly experience divine love is gratitude (even after the Turkey\u2019s done & Thanksgiving is over!). And gratitude isn\u2019t just one way to experience divine love for the first time, or to get it back when the feeling fades. I think it\u2019s essential if we want to live in that love instead of occasionally visiting it.\n\nThis is a bit tricky to put into words, but let me try anyway\u2026 You may be suffering now, but you could be suffering more. That\u2019s something to be grateful for. I\u2019m not talking just about changing your \u201cglass half empty\u201d perception to a glass \u201chalf full perception.\u201d I\u2019m not pushing \u201ctoxic positivity.\u201d And I\u2019m certainly not preaching \u201cprosperity philosophy,\" even though it can seem to start at that. I'm ultimately reaching for something much deeper.\n\nIf you have a moment right now to breathe, that\u2019s something to be grateful for. I learned this many times the hard way. For example, when I got tuberculosis from a cave in Mexico at age 20, and coughed for six months straight, eventually literally unable to breathe, leading to a near death experience. And then in Senegal, I had a suspected bilharzia infection in 2021, and could only painfully pee blood, I promised to always be thankful if I could ever pee normally again. Miraculously, I then found some obscure ethnobotanical research (on [Google Scholar](http://scholar.google.com), before AI) which led me to drink roselle tea. Within an hour, I was cured. I don\u2019t always remember to say \u201cthank you\u201d when I pee now, but I try.\n\nThat may sound like a silly, quirky waste of time to some folks. But what I notice is that saying thank you for every little thing, whenever I remember to, is not about imposing some kind of \u201cspiritual discipline.\u201d It is directly reconnecting me to something pure and sacred. And even (or especially) when I am suffering, I try to remember to be grateful for that also, because if not for suffering, I would not even think about how much I have enjoyed not suffering as much, and how much it will help me to develop more compassion, wisdom, and gratitude in future. It connects me with the plight of the countless other beings suffering at that very moment, authentically bringing up a wellspring of compassion in my heart for all of us. And I see clearly that it is my inner child who is feeling this pain, and what an honor it is that I can be there for him.\n\nOf course, that\u2019s not to say you should not try to suffer less. The point of life, according to every spiritual tradition and religion, I think, is to suffer less, which first requires us to understand the cause of suffering, and what real suffering and real happiness are. If you, like Biggie Smalls, have come to understand that having money is not real happiness, that\u2019s a big deal! That\u2019s something to be grateful for.\n\nNotice how, when you offer thanks (to the universe, to life, to God, whatever you may believe in), a space in your heart opens up. It might not feel immediately like you suddenly know the real meaning of Divine Love, but it\u2019s a little spark of \u2026 something.... There\u2019s a tiny bit of freedom, of peace, stillness, softness. And as that softness deepens each time, you start to understand what I mean by Divine Love. Many people have written books on this topic (like [A. Helwa\u2019s book](https://authorahelwa.com/), Secrets of Divine Love, for example), so I won\u2019t belabor the point further here, but I hope you are able to experience this when you try it out for a while, and I\u2019d really love to know how it works for you!\n\nOnce you start to feel that lovely softness of the heart, invite your little one in with you as your spiritual integration practice. They might go numb, or call BS on that. If so, they might just need to see if you're actually humble enough, and grateful enough for them, to listen to their rejection and be patient. If you instead retreat back into your cozy corner, it's the food bank feast all over again: You can have an enormous spiritual feast upstairs while the child is still starving downstairs.\n\n### When the Spiritual Relationship Hurts\n\nIf a spiritual teacher you trusted betrayed you, even sitting down to meditate can feel like it belongs to them now. Kids quit piano over one bad teacher, and this is way worse. But if you let an abuser control your appreciation of life, then you've given them an unearned win.\n\nBeing the grown-up doesn't mean controlling everything yourself, though. After a betrayal like that, part of you might want to. Spiritual support can help you act where it's yours to act, and let go of outcomes and other people's choices where it isn't. The [Serenity Prayer](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Serenity_Prayer) is the example I'd give, if prayer still feels like yours.\n\n## Become the Adult Apprentice\n\nSay a friend sits with you while you fall apart. Most of us feel better and call them again the next time, and they get a little better at it every time while we don't. So steal some of it. Usually what helped was how they just sat there without trying to fix you. Ten seconds of sitting with yourself like that is good enough for a start, so try it before you call next time.", "483856877cf44be810bb6e85232606de41c0ce0f4bd44fb2f5fd4be683c0627d"], ["t55_unt_h2", () => "## Untangling the Belonging Bargains\n\nPart of building who you are is untangling yourself from whatever pulls you away from your own knowing in return for belonging. A political tribe might expect consistent positions and predictable reactions, while a family or religion that's kind to you might have expectations that just feel like being good. Most groups expect some conformity, which is ordinary. Your family might want a particular kind of son or daughter, and your friends might have rules about what you can joke about or get upset about. But if going along means betraying yourself, that's the bargain.\n\nThen there's another bargain, where you have to earn your worth: when you don't feel your value inside, you can become a \"human doer,\" working for it in some arena outside yourself. That might be beauty, athleticism, sexual desirability, intelligence or grades. For some people it's money or status, and for others it's a sophisticated spiritual identity or becoming indispensable to everybody else's problems. It can feel really good to be the one everybody calls. In the more shadowy presentation, you can take that caretaking role too far and end up keeping other people dependent, because being needed has become your main source of value.\n\nThese can all be real gifts. The bargain begins when your worth depends on them, or when you feel you have to keep performing them to be loved. Your little one can hang onto those bargains as if belonging were a matter of life or death, especially if you haven't learned to parent him or her yourself yet. Even with good parents, you could have learned this when people repeatedly praised what you did rather than your inherent worth. That wasn't your flaw. Self-differentiation is learning to notice when you're making that trade, and to keep your own ground instead.\n\n### Four Pairs That Commonly Hook the Child\n\nPema Ch\u00f6dr\u00f6n has a quick map for finding the bargain. It's four pairs, starting with pleasure and pain, and gain and loss. The other two are about what people think of you: praise and blame, and good reputation and disgrace. Your little one can panic when something hurts, and also when something good might disappear, like praise, romance, money, status, belonging or a spiritual high. You might still be enjoying one of those when your little one starts asking how long this is going to last.\n\nWhen that happens, you can ask yourself these, once you've caught your breath:\n\n- What do I think I'm about to get, or lose?\n- Whose approval suddenly feels like I can't live without it? It isn't always who you'd guess.\n- Is this a value I'm standing up for, or a high I'm trying to keep?\n- Would this still be something I'd feel is worth doing if at the end of the day, I didn't get praise from anyone for it?\n\nYou don't have to stop enjoying pleasure, praise, gain or belonging. The question is whether losing one of them would feel like losing yourself.\n\nDifferentiation can happen right inside a family, religion, community or relationship. You'd be belonging selectively, which still leaves room to belong. As a differentiated adult you can stay deeply embedded, free to question things or disagree. If a parent used to go quiet and hurt whenever you disagreed, that might be hard to believe. A family where everyone gets to be their own person is healthier than an enmeshed one, and a faith that's your own is healthier than religious compliance. It can be the same religion either way. And two differentiated people have a sturdier relationship than two who've been absorbed into each other.\n\nDay to day, differentiation looks something like this:\n\n- Caring about somebody's feelings without automatically adopting their opinions or sacrificing your own ground. Their reasons can still change your mind, though.\n- Noticing when you agreed in the moment and disagree later: \"Wait, why was I nodding along?\"\n- Noticing when what you thought you knew suddenly feels like water dripping through your hand because of praise or pity, fear or attraction, or something that really resonates with you.\n- Disappointing people and withstanding social pressure, and still being recognizably yourself wherever you are (maybe louder in some places). That one can take a while.\n- Questioning what you were programmed to believe while holding the discomfort, and without having to stop loving anyone over it.\n\nDifferentiation is slow, and it goes on your whole life. If a family, group or relationship won't let you do this while you're still part of it, temporary distance may be necessary. Some people come back later on different terms. Sometimes the leaving turns out to be permanent, because going back would mean disappearing again. You don't have to know yet which one yours is. As you differentiate, you learn to tell the difference.\n\nKey developed this identity and differentiation portion of the method, drawing on Murray Bowen's family-systems work and her own self-therapy.", "26bd798b29cf8428a1e3193061383d1b3c01d92b1a96a4b0f4cc1b671243fd16"], ["t55_play_h2", () => "## Let the Child Be Bad at Things\n\nReflection can show you what got sMothered (typo?) or suppressed. For identity you also need some experiments out in real life, so go try something new just for you. Maybe a foraging group, or a painting class. Stargazing can be awe-inspiring, and you might even see a UFO! Notice what holds your attention when nobody is impressed.\n\nThe old parent in your head may demand immediate talent, though, and then the first time you're awkward at it, that's \"evidence\" that you're stupid or clumsy. Or you hear, \"Come on, you're not really the type.\" I think your little one is listening to see if anybody's going to stick up for them. The grown-up you has to be the one who does: \"It's okay if we're bad at this, nobody's on trial. We're just seeing if we like it.\"\n\nGive a new interest enough tries to get past the humiliation of being a beginner, when you keep telling people, \"Sorry, I'm new.\" Maybe after that you still genuinely dislike it, and then you may quit. You may also discover a part of yourself that never had permission to exist. Either way, that's pl/ork in its most literal form: play becomes part of forming your identity. You might catch yourself wondering when you became someone who does this.", "1cd9021ac0d005f75eb66bab873d01e2713d7f96143370fcf7304ac8fda3296c"], ["t55_some_h1", () => "# Sometimes There Isn't a Clear Child Yet\n\nReparenting assumes there's a clear little one in there to love, and for a lot of people there is. But if you grew up somewhere you only belonged as long as you played somebody else, like a really controlling family or a strict church, the real one may never have had room to form. All this stuff about your little one could have left you wondering who I'm even talking about.\n\nMaybe you started playing that part so early, and it took over so completely, that \"What do I believe?\" or \"What do I want?\" has no clear answer of your own. They sound like such easy questions, too. Back then, asking might have gotten you in trouble, or laughed at. Or there was just no way to ask. That can make \"love yourself\" feel pretty empty (love what, when the part you've been playing doesn't feel real?).\n\n## From Survival to Experimental Play\n\nIf your childhood meant avoiding pain and keeping the people you needed close, your attention got used up reading danger (was that sigh about me?) and meeting their expectations, and when curiosity or mistakes got you punished, or it felt like they loved you less, you got better at the part. That left much less room for experiments that show you what you like when nobody's grading you. People grow into who they are by trying things on and then changing their minds, from what they wear down to the ideas and roles they take up, like a teenager who starts spelling her name with an \"i\" at the end and later changes it back.\n\nSome of this is developmental pl/ork you get to do late: making it safe enough for your curiosity and experimenting to finally grow, and your preferences too. It might feel wrong somehow to like something before you've checked anyone's face. You can usually reparent and build who you are at the same time.\n\nAs you tell the part you've been playing apart from the self that didn't get much practice, notice whether it makes you more curious about both, and better able to relate to them. Or does it have you thinking, \"That one's fake, and I'm getting rid of it\"? You're after a relationship between them inside you that works better, and that's hard to have if there's a new split between an acceptable self and one that has to disappear.\n\nWhen you ask what your little one feels, maybe all you get is a guess, which isn't proof there's no little one in there. Maybe you hear yourself answer, \"Scared? I mean, it would make sense if it was scared.\"\n\nSo early on, the practice is finding out who's actually in there, through long reflection with no outside input, and journaling just for yourself, without picturing a reader. You might catch yourself explaining who everybody is anyway. Time alone in nature helps too, and so does getting back the preferences that got buried: the foods you really like and what feels good to do, and what you believe and feel when nobody's around to take your cues from. Focusing (Eugene Gendlin's method of listening to a vague, felt sense in your body) can especially help you notice feelings and preferences the part you've been playing learned to overlook. Any words you put to them can stay tentative (\"I hate cooking... or just for everybody?\") while you look at what they seem to mean and revise how you read them.\n\n## Untangling the Belonging Bargains\n\nPart of building who you are is untangling yourself from whatever pulls you away from your own knowing in return for belonging. A political tribe might expect consistent positions and predictable reactions, while a family or religion that's kind to you might have expectations that just feel like being good. Most groups expect some conformity, which is ordinary. Your family might want a particular kind of son or daughter, and your friends might have rules about what you can joke about or get upset about. But if going along means betraying yourself, that's the bargain.\n\nThen there's another bargain, where you have to earn your worth: when you don't feel your value inside, you can become a \"human doer,\" working for it in some arena outside yourself. That might be beauty, athleticism, sexual desirability, intelligence or grades. For some people it's money or status, and for others it's a sophisticated spiritual identity or becoming indispensable to everybody else's problems. It can feel really good to be the one everybody calls. In the more shadowy presentation, you can take that caretaking role too far and end up keeping other people dependent, because being needed has become your main source of value.\n\nThese can all be real gifts. The bargain begins when your worth depends on them, or when you feel you have to keep performing them to be loved. Your little one can hang onto those bargains as if belonging were a matter of life or death, especially if you haven't learned to parent him or her yourself yet. Even with good parents, you could have learned this when people repeatedly praised what you did rather than your inherent worth. That wasn't your flaw. Self-differentiation is learning to notice when you're making that trade, and to keep your own ground instead.\n\n### Four Pairs That Commonly Hook the Child\n\nPema Ch\u00f6dr\u00f6n has a quick map for finding the bargain. It's four pairs, starting with pleasure and pain, and gain and loss. The other two are about what people think of you: praise and blame, and good reputation and disgrace. Your little one can panic when something hurts, and also when something good might disappear, like praise, romance, money, status, belonging or a spiritual high. You might still be enjoying one of those when your little one starts asking how long this is going to last.\n\nWhen that happens, you can ask yourself these, once you've caught your breath:\n\n- What do I think I'm about to get, or lose?\n- Whose approval suddenly feels like I can't live without it? It isn't always who you'd guess.\n- Is this a value I'm standing up for, or a high I'm trying to keep?\n- Would this still be something I'd feel is worth doing if at the end of the day, I didn't get praise from anyone for it?\n\nYou don't have to stop enjoying pleasure, praise, gain or belonging. The question is whether losing one of them would feel like losing yourself.\n\nDifferentiation can happen right inside a family, religion, community or relationship. You'd be belonging selectively, which still leaves room to belong. As a differentiated adult you can stay deeply embedded, free to question things or disagree. If a parent used to go quiet and hurt whenever you disagreed, that might be hard to believe. A family where everyone gets to be their own person is healthier than an enmeshed one, and a faith that's your own is healthier than religious compliance. It can be the same religion either way. And two differentiated people have a sturdier relationship than two who've been absorbed into each other.\n\nDay to day, differentiation looks something like this:\n\n- Caring about somebody's feelings without automatically adopting their opinions or sacrificing your own ground. Their reasons can still change your mind, though.\n- Noticing when you agreed in the moment and disagree later: \"Wait, why was I nodding along?\"\n- Noticing when what you thought you knew suddenly feels like water dripping through your hand because of praise or pity, fear or attraction, or something that really resonates with you.\n- Disappointing people and withstanding social pressure, and still being recognizably yourself wherever you are (maybe louder in some places). That one can take a while.\n- Questioning what you were programmed to believe while holding the discomfort, and without having to stop loving anyone over it.\n\nDifferentiation is slow, and it goes on your whole life. If a family, group or relationship won't let you do this while you're still part of it, temporary distance may be necessary. Some people come back later on different terms. Sometimes the leaving turns out to be permanent, because going back would mean disappearing again. You don't have to know yet which one yours is. As you differentiate, you learn to tell the difference.\n\nKey developed this identity and differentiation portion of the method, drawing on Murray Bowen's family-systems work and her own self-therapy.\n\n## Let the Child Be Bad at Things\n\nReflection can show you what got sMothered (typo?) or suppressed. For identity you also need some experiments out in real life, so go try something new just for you. Maybe a foraging group, or a painting class. Stargazing can be awe-inspiring, and you might even see a UFO! Notice what holds your attention when nobody is impressed.\n\nThe old parent in your head may demand immediate talent, though, and then the first time you're awkward at it, that's \"evidence\" that you're stupid or clumsy. Or you hear, \"Come on, you're not really the type.\" I think your little one is listening to see if anybody's going to stick up for them. The grown-up you has to be the one who does: \"It's okay if we're bad at this, nobody's on trial. We're just seeing if we like it.\"\n\nGive a new interest enough tries to get past the humiliation of being a beginner, when you keep telling people, \"Sorry, I'm new.\" Maybe after that you still genuinely dislike it, and then you may quit. You may also discover a part of yourself that never had permission to exist. Either way, that's pl/ork in its most literal form: play becomes part of forming your identity. You might catch yourself wondering when you became someone who does this.", "fb1b706898d146edebef57463c794d03fee62e1d4c51f74e59d53af85b3b328f"], ["t55_heal_h3", () => "### When Healing Turns Into Checking\n\nYou can get hooked on healing too. The wound can be completely real while you're checking it for the hundredth time to see whether you've finally grieved it correctly.\n\nGoing back can still matter, since realizations are a real part of life. But returning to the same wound can also become a search for certainty in an inherently uncertain existence. Spending all day asking, \"Did I process it properly? Did I grieve it enough?\" is clearly not what we're after here.\n\nIf you're feeling guilty because you didn't put your seat belt on, just put it on. If you still want to investigate it later, fine. Perhaps now and then you feel bad about that time you refused to put it on, broke your collar bone in a crash, and then got blamed for not wearing it. That's natural, but doesn't mean there's something new to look at. Then the background checker goes, \u201cBut are you really listening?\u201d There it is. Seat belt's on. \ud83d\ude07 Where were we?\n\nDon't be afraid to ask your therapist/yourself \"What is this practice supposed to help, and how would I know if it's helping?\" This sovereign, gently curious stance should apply to everything in life. Feeling calmer or having deeper insights or explanations for the trauma response are some possible signs of initial progress, but if that's the endgame, it's just a cope. Real healing is ultimately measured by reduced need for the therapy. It's like real parenting: you might start out reading parenting books and thinking hard about what to say or do, but after a while you get into the groove and a lot of it becomes natural. Inner parenting can be like that too. You don't need to keep checking this guide or planning every conversation with your little one; the nurturing, protecting and guiding become more automatic. You might still turn to therapy when you need it, and life will keep offering chances to grow wiser and more compassionate. And if the therapy is actually working, it should improve your daily life, and especially the health of the inner child-inner adult relationship. Going at it harder isn't as useful as going at it with more wisdom and compassion. Take the parts that work, and innovate on the rest.\n\nIf these exercises start feeling like a ritual you have to complete before you're allowed to live, stop there.", "1bd59e8e47275b6cdbbf47748693866bf297e64635ebe7ca1628ce8ff69d45d6"], ["t55_body_h2", () => "## Your Body Might Need Some Love First\n\nIf you notice tension in your solar plexus or abdomen, or anywhere else that seems to be keeping you from relaxing into that heart chakra love, try some non-forced, slower exhales and a little gentle self-massage. I love using one foot to massage the other foot like a yin-yang symbol, but you can start with some nice circular or spiral massage right on the tense spot.\n\nIf that's not enough, there's deeper somatic stuff like [Shaking](https://www.skool.com/shakingmedicine/about?ref=ee69b2178e96491f88530789dc160291), tapping (EFT), EMDR, Brainspotting, Somatic Experiencing, Water Therapy, Massage, Energy Healing, or some other bottom-up approach.\n\nWhen your nervous system is flooded, your protective parts are busy with the flood, so they're not going to let your little one come out and talk much anyway. Some people go straight into EMDR on their worst memory before they can even stay in the room. Then they get flooded all over again and decide EMDR doesn't work. Here's a map of what to try when:\n\nWhatever you try from that map, I wouldn't go just by how pleasant a session felt to tell whether it was too much. This kind of work can bring up grief and fear, and sometimes hard dreams, or stuff you'd rather never have met. Even staying present the whole time doesn't tell you enough by itself. It's a bit like the gym, where sore and injured can feel pretty similar that evening, and it's the next few days that tell you which one it was.\n\nSo how were the next few days? How did you sleep, and could you get through your day okay? Do you want to go back toward what came up, or is some part of you saying no? (And was it hard but doable, or just too much? A lot of the time you just know.) If you honestly can't tell yet, I wouldn't rush to call it healing or harm. I'd just make the next one a bit easier, one change at a time, so you learn what made the difference. If you're getting worse, though, change several things at once, and get some support.\n\nBring the challenge back gradually, and only when you want to and you're recovering well. Some people try to toughen themselves up in a cauldron, but what you're looking for is the edge where you can actually learn. And what about those hard dreams, or images that pop up during a session? They can show what feels important or frightening right now. Sometimes they show what feels missing, or what feels needed. They aren't evidence that what you saw in them happened, though, and shouldn't be used to confirm a memory or rebuild one. Or to suggest one.\n\n### Catch the Hook Before the Story Takes Over\n\nPema Ch\u00f6dr\u00f6n calls the tightening and urge you get before the story has finished forming \"shenpa,\" and she says \"[hooked](https://www.lionsroar.com/how-we-get-hooked-shenpa-and-how-we-get-unhooked/)\" is a more descriptive translation than the usual \"attachment.\" You might notice the hook before you have any clue what part of you is doing the hooking. Or you might notice it embarrassingly late. The jaw and stomach are already tight, and/or half the text is written, and then suddenly it dawns on you: you're supposed to be observing yourself. \ud83d\ude00 Maybe forget the parts detective work for a minute. You can figure that out later.\n\nWhen you catch it, look around and just take a moment to enjoy some free honesty: \"Oh! I'm hooked again! That's awesome I could notice it!\" If nobody is in danger, take a breath or three before you finish whatever you were doing. Sometimes human beings need to be being, not always doing. \ud83d\ude43 Maybe then you'll find there's enough adult around to be nice to your little boo for a second. Or enough Protector to stop something you'd regret later.\n\nHere's where I want to clearly depart from the new-age wizdumb that all your problems are self-created: Somebody may really have crossed a boundary. That's not just because they are your karmic partner mirroring what God wants to show you in yourself, even if that also applies sometimes. Your reaction is what you can control the most, though. If what they did hit something old in you, notice how that feels in your body. And notice how your feelings can also amplify or distort your perceptions of others. Just because your stomach tightened up doesn't mean their boundary crossing indicates \u201cThey're evil.\u201d You can defer that judgement for when you're in your happy place.\n\nFeelings about yourself work the same way. \"I feel attracted\" or \"I feel ashamed\" is real as a feeling, but it doesn't settle who you are, or who you love. Making too much out of something momentary is one side of the human psyche's pendulum. Give yourself time to see what patterns form more durably, and what feels authentically yours vs something planted or manipulated into being there from society, influencers, family, friends, religion, etc., etc. You may find an exceptional freedom in exactly that in-between place where you don't have to decide what your pronouns are, or whether you believe in this or that. If you stay in flux forever, though, it can seem like there's nothing solid about you. In that case, sometimes it helps to meditate or talk with a good unbiased therapist who can help you see the pattern.\n\nThe opposite extreme of our pendulum, throwing everything transient away, as some people do with \"thought-noting\" practice, also isn't healthy, except as a meditation exercise. Transient feelings and thoughts can be meaningful signals. For example, if you don't want to be touched, say no now and work out what it means later. Same if something might be dangerous right now, or might be a medical warning sign: step away or get checked first.\n\n### Also Look Outward\n\nOnce you're in your happy place, look at the other person too, and don't go only by the one fight where they crossed your boundary, or, the other way around, by the one \"no\" or boundary of theirs that made you mad \u2014 after all, they're allowed to say no to you. Does it keep ending the same way, where you bring up something that hurt and then still end up reassuring them they're not a bad person, and the talk never comes back to what happened to you? (The psychologist [Lindsay Gibson](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=VlNpgFWOLPw) wrote the book on this: [*Disentangling from Emotionally Immature People*](https://www.newharbinger.com/9781648481512/disentangling-from-emotionally-immature-people/).) Or can they hear you disagree and look at their own part?\n\nIf it's the first, it can seem like you haven't explained yourself well enough yet. You probably have, and it makes more sense to count on what they keep showing you they can do than on the right words finally getting through to them. Maybe what they can do is text you something kind the next day and not bring any of it up.\n\nYou may not know for certain whether the other person is or could be capable of a healthier response, and neither do they. If they promise to change, that's probably as useful as their prior promises were. Think about how long it has taken you to become more healthy. Could you have done that from one moment to the next based on a promise? Sometimes we can be a better support for someone when we maintain a realistic boundary, so that we can hold high expectations, and still feel prepared for their failure to meet them.\n\nhttps://substack.com/profile/355021294-u-dont-existcom/note/c-349048098\n\nIf [a relationship](https://romance.u-dont-exist.com) keeps making you feel less safe, meditate on why it makes you feel unsafe, and what keeps you in it. Might it be due to a lack of the healed inner child/inner adult relationship? (If so, you're reading the right guide!) But if they've actually hurt or threatened you, even once, old stuff or not, get safe first, which is not a small thing at all, I know.\n\nIf you really did hurt them, repair it. Also easier said than done, but...\n\nYou can mean the apology for a start. Then notice what happens next. Are you sorry for what you did, or are you starting to take back the complaint too because they're still upset? Maybe your first explanation sucked. Try again. \ud83d\ude05 And if it fails again, and you've said what you mean, well, you can't guarantee they'll understand it. Guilt may keep shouting at you. That doesn't tell you the whole conflict was your fault, nor does it prove they're manipulating you.\n\nThen there are people you're just getting to know. Meeting them can turn into a kind of test--\"First impressions are everything,\" as they say, but it could be even worse than that. If the real reason you're asking someone out is to prove you're lovable, or that you're sure what you want, then they've become part of the test. I'm giving you permission now to quit school and flunk the tests. Get curious about who this person is, and who you are, and let both of you have your own reasons for being there in the beautiful present.\n\nIf being alone was a big part of what hurt in the first place, then reaching toward people is part of taking care of your little one too. Do you have to get yourself sorted out first, so you'll be better company? I really don't think so. If the hurt was being alone, waiting just means more of being alone. So I'd call somebody or show up somewhere, mess and all.\n\nAnd reaching out doesn't mean telling strangers everything. Privacy isn't fakery. People can earn more of you by how they handle the smaller things you tell them, and how they take it when you say no. But if a relationship is getting deep and you're still keeping up a fake normal, happy version of yourself to hold onto it, I'd either bring more of the truth in, carefully, or let it be less deep for now. (If telling could put you in real danger, like violence, or being outed where that isn't safe, protect yourself first.) And when someone finally accepts you, it can feel amazing. It still doesn't prove you're meant for each other, or that they've earned your trust yet.\n\nI can't tell you where to look for people who fit you, but I'd bet on more than one place. Keep the good people you've already got. Then maybe try a class, or a spiritual community if one really fits you. Not every one of them has to click (nobody's taking attendance). And if any group, or any one person, starts acting like they should be your only one, that's where I'd want the Protector paying attention.\n\n### Write It. Don't Send It Yet.\n\nIf you've got something burning you up that you wanna say, write it out as a reply. There. That got it out. Ahh. Breathing easier. \ud83d\ude0c But don't send it. Take that breather you earned and then come back to it from a cooler place.\n\nIt doesn't mean you need to hide your upset, or apply for a permit to protest, but choose your battles and execute them wisely, so you don't end up regretting how you communicated later. As Lao Tsu might have said (if I could ever have read that whole boring Art of War book), a war with no casualties is the best possible war. When high IQ & EQ communication hits a writer's block, sometimes the best thing is to simply say, \"I need some time to think about my response.\" And if the conflict really requires a response right now, then obviously just do your best rather than wait for the perfect timing.\n\nAnd yeah, going back to the writer, which is often my inner child when emotions pop up, is part of the process. It's like I'm holding his hand and showing him a better way, not like I just stole his pencil because he wrote something embarrassing. So what was he actually trying to say? A lot of the time it's just \"that hurt.\"\n\nOther times it's \"I did this too.\" My friend Tyler saw a guy smoking in front of a baby and asked him if he thought that was wise. The guy asked if he thought it was wise to mind his own business, and Tyler said, \"Well, I believe in Christ, so I thought you might care about life too.\" Afterward he wasn't sure he'd handled it right, or whether he should have thrown the first stone at all, since he'd smoked in front of a baby himself.\n\nBut Jesus also said to take the log out of your own eye first, and then \"[you will see clearly to take the speck out of your brother's eye](https://www.biblegateway.com/passage/?search=Matthew%207%3A3-5&version=ESV).\" That's exactly what made Tyler the right person to say something. If you actually have the problem they have, and you want to call them out for it, call yourself out for it instead. What we came up with was something like, \"Hey man, when I was younger I smoked in front of an infant, not realizing how selfish that was, and I notice you doing the same thing. I'd ask you to please have some compassion for him. I have a young child now, so this really touches me.\"\n\nIt's also how your voice sounds, though. You can say all the right words, but if the tone is accusatory, it doesn't matter. Tyler's an evangelical Christian who preaches on YouTube, so it meant a lot to me that he liked this take on what Jesus was saying.\n\n### When Healing Turns Into Checking\n\nYou can get hooked on healing too. The wound can be completely real while you're checking it for the hundredth time to see whether you've finally grieved it correctly.\n\nGoing back can still matter, since realizations are a real part of life. But returning to the same wound can also become a search for certainty in an inherently uncertain existence. Spending all day asking, \"Did I process it properly? Did I grieve it enough?\" is clearly not what we're after here.\n\nIf you're feeling guilty because you didn't put your seat belt on, just put it on. If you still want to investigate it later, fine. Perhaps now and then you feel bad about that time you refused to put it on, broke your collar bone in a crash, and then got blamed for not wearing it. That's natural, but doesn't mean there's something new to look at. Then the background checker goes, \u201cBut are you really listening?\u201d There it is. Seat belt's on. \ud83d\ude07 Where were we?\n\nDon't be afraid to ask your therapist/yourself \"What is this practice supposed to help, and how would I know if it's helping?\" This sovereign, gently curious stance should apply to everything in life. Feeling calmer or having deeper insights or explanations for the trauma response are some possible signs of initial progress, but if that's the endgame, it's just a cope. Real healing is ultimately measured by reduced need for the therapy. It's like real parenting: you might start out reading parenting books and thinking hard about what to say or do, but after a while you get into the groove and a lot of it becomes natural. Inner parenting can be like that too. You don't need to keep checking this guide or planning every conversation with your little one; the nurturing, protecting and guiding become more automatic. You might still turn to therapy when you need it, and life will keep offering chances to grow wiser and more compassionate. And if the therapy is actually working, it should improve your daily life, and especially the health of the inner child-inner adult relationship. Going at it harder isn't as useful as going at it with more wisdom and compassion. Take the parts that work, and innovate on the rest.\n\nIf these exercises start feeling like a ritual you have to complete before you're allowed to live, stop there.", "363fa7469f623568169beedb8f9b08801b570ad9c0d46b90aa7a16a48db427dd"], ["t55_before_h1", () => "# Before You Try to Go Deep\n\nHow much of the grown-up you is actually here right now? Try looking around the room, pausing before you act, making a choice and following through on it. If you can't do that because you're basically the frightened kid, leave the deeper conversation until later. You may need somebody steady there with you, or to do whatever helps you come back a bit before you try to talk to the child.\n\nAnd if the grown-up you is there, and it's just how deep you're going that's too much, I'd go shallower. Some people try one technique after another instead, to prove to themselves they can take it. Your little one may have had to just take it back then, though. Now there's a grown-up around who can say, \"Okay, that's enough for today,\" and try something gentler instead of giving up on them. Even just saying hi to them with your eyes open, before any imagery, counts.\n\nIf you ever need a full break from your little one, I'd jot down why, and how far you'd gotten before it got to be too much. A couple of lines on your phone is plenty. After a while away, it gets hard to remember. So once that reason has changed and you want to come back, the note tells you where it was still okay. I'd start there, or somewhere a bit gentler.\n\n## When the Present-Day Adult Is Dangerous to the Child\n\nI've been writing this whole guide as if everybody who tries it wants to take care of the inner child. Some people get a little rush out of making somebody scared or small, though. For such folks, inner child therapy is clearly not the place to start. They need to first deconstruct the adult identity they've got.\n\nWhen I talk with folks like that, I mostly want to know what the identity gets them and what it costs, and especially what it's really serving. Some of them even call themselves sovereign, and then somehow there's an urge they have to obey, which sounds more like slavery to me.\n\nThis part tends to scare the wrong people, like somebody who's horrified by their own thoughts. Everybody gets scary thoughts they'd rather not have sometimes, and they don't make you a bad person, but why would you bring your inner child into the middle of them? The nice thing about inner child therapy is that you can wait until you're calm and actually ready. A real parent doesn't get to do that, which is one reason nobody manages to be a perfect one.\n\nAnd if somebody actually wonders whether they're malicious, they'd do better to meditate on it and find out why they're asking before they bring their inner child anywhere near it. Are they really feeling malicious, or is it some voice in their head saying so, like the inherited critic? If some of it turns out to be malice they actually want to act on, the inner child can wait.\n\nIn one of the Buddha's own accounts of how he woke up, ill will shows up in his mind and he sees it's hurting him. If he had to figure that out by watching, it's not obvious. Ill will can make you feel powerful. Being on fire would too, and people would definitely get out of your way, but unless you're the Human Torch from the Fantastic Four, you'd be the one burning. So it's worth noticing what it's doing to you. Has it ever left anybody at ease, with nothing to regret afterward? Of course not.\n\nThe Buddha also told his son that loving-kindness is what gets rid of ill will, and compassion does the same for cruelty. I'm biased on loving-kindness, since it got me out of my depression. For these folks it starts with the grown-up they are now, though, and their little one stays out of it.\n\nAs for when their little one gets a turn, I'd say once they've had some bad days where scaring somebody would've felt good, and they didn't do it. Until then it helps a lot to have a real person in their life who likes them and won't laugh along, since loving-kindness is easier to pick up from someone who already has some.\n\n## You Don\u2019t Need an Inner Monologue\n\nIf you don't hear a little kid talking in your head, don't invent one. Actually, this is one place where the usual inner-child prompt to \u201cask your little one what they need\u201d can get confusing, because \u201cask\u201d sounds like you're supposed to ask a question and then wait for a second voice to answer. Maybe that's what happens for some people, but you might not be some people (hehe).\n\nYou might barely have spontaneous words in your mind and yet still be able to think a sentence on purpose. Try it and see. If words are there, speak or write them. You might feel more comfortable that way than just staying in imagination land.  And if that starts feeling like homework you're doing for a child who isn't answering, drop it. Maybe what's actually requiring your attention now is a knot in your stomach. Or perhaps you're in a place you want to leave. Sometimes nothing \u201canswers\u201d and you just know what would be nice to do for yourself.\n\nAnd it might not be the same order all the time. You could have a feeling before thoughts, or thoughts might come first and provoke a feeling. They say hindsight is 20/20, but you actually might not even know which happened first. That's ok, too.\n\n## Your Body Might Need Some Love First\n\nIf you notice tension in your solar plexus or abdomen, or anywhere else that seems to be keeping you from relaxing into that heart chakra love, try some non-forced, slower exhales and a little gentle self-massage. I love using one foot to massage the other foot like a yin-yang symbol, but you can start with some nice circular or spiral massage right on the tense spot.\n\nIf that's not enough, there's deeper somatic stuff like [Shaking](https://www.skool.com/shakingmedicine/about?ref=ee69b2178e96491f88530789dc160291), tapping (EFT), EMDR, Brainspotting, Somatic Experiencing, Water Therapy, Massage, Energy Healing, or some other bottom-up approach.\n\nWhen your nervous system is flooded, your protective parts are busy with the flood, so they're not going to let your little one come out and talk much anyway. Some people go straight into EMDR on their worst memory before they can even stay in the room. Then they get flooded all over again and decide EMDR doesn't work. Here's a map of what to try when:\n\nWhatever you try from that map, I wouldn't go just by how pleasant a session felt to tell whether it was too much. This kind of work can bring up grief and fear, and sometimes hard dreams, or stuff you'd rather never have met. Even staying present the whole time doesn't tell you enough by itself. It's a bit like the gym, where sore and injured can feel pretty similar that evening, and it's the next few days that tell you which one it was.\n\nSo how were the next few days? How did you sleep, and could you get through your day okay? Do you want to go back toward what came up, or is some part of you saying no? (And was it hard but doable, or just too much? A lot of the time you just know.) If you honestly can't tell yet, I wouldn't rush to call it healing or harm. I'd just make the next one a bit easier, one change at a time, so you learn what made the difference. If you're getting worse, though, change several things at once, and get some support.\n\nBring the challenge back gradually, and only when you want to and you're recovering well. Some people try to toughen themselves up in a cauldron, but what you're looking for is the edge where you can actually learn. And what about those hard dreams, or images that pop up during a session? They can show what feels important or frightening right now. Sometimes they show what feels missing, or what feels needed. They aren't evidence that what you saw in them happened, though, and shouldn't be used to confirm a memory or rebuild one. Or to suggest one.\n\n### Catch the Hook Before the Story Takes Over\n\nPema Ch\u00f6dr\u00f6n calls the tightening and urge you get before the story has finished forming \"shenpa,\" and she says \"[hooked](https://www.lionsroar.com/how-we-get-hooked-shenpa-and-how-we-get-unhooked/)\" is a more descriptive translation than the usual \"attachment.\" You might notice the hook before you have any clue what part of you is doing the hooking. Or you might notice it embarrassingly late. The jaw and stomach are already tight, and/or half the text is written, and then suddenly it dawns on you: you're supposed to be observing yourself. \ud83d\ude00 Maybe forget the parts detective work for a minute. You can figure that out later.\n\nWhen you catch it, look around and just take a moment to enjoy some free honesty: \"Oh! I'm hooked again! That's awesome I could notice it!\" If nobody is in danger, take a breath or three before you finish whatever you were doing. Sometimes human beings need to be being, not always doing. \ud83d\ude43 Maybe then you'll find there's enough adult around to be nice to your little boo for a second. Or enough Protector to stop something you'd regret later.\n\nHere's where I want to clearly depart from the new-age wizdumb that all your problems are self-created: Somebody may really have crossed a boundary. That's not just because they are your karmic partner mirroring what God wants to show you in yourself, even if that also applies sometimes. Your reaction is what you can control the most, though. If what they did hit something old in you, notice how that feels in your body. And notice how your feelings can also amplify or distort your perceptions of others. Just because your stomach tightened up doesn't mean their boundary crossing indicates \u201cThey're evil.\u201d You can defer that judgement for when you're in your happy place.\n\nFeelings about yourself work the same way. \"I feel attracted\" or \"I feel ashamed\" is real as a feeling, but it doesn't settle who you are, or who you love. Making too much out of something momentary is one side of the human psyche's pendulum. Give yourself time to see what patterns form more durably, and what feels authentically yours vs something planted or manipulated into being there from society, influencers, family, friends, religion, etc., etc. You may find an exceptional freedom in exactly that in-between place where you don't have to decide what your pronouns are, or whether you believe in this or that. If you stay in flux forever, though, it can seem like there's nothing solid about you. In that case, sometimes it helps to meditate or talk with a good unbiased therapist who can help you see the pattern.\n\nThe opposite extreme of our pendulum, throwing everything transient away, as some people do with \"thought-noting\" practice, also isn't healthy, except as a meditation exercise. Transient feelings and thoughts can be meaningful signals. For example, if you don't want to be touched, say no now and work out what it means later. Same if something might be dangerous right now, or might be a medical warning sign: step away or get checked first.\n\n### Also Look Outward\n\nOnce you're in your happy place, look at the other person too, and don't go only by the one fight where they crossed your boundary, or, the other way around, by the one \"no\" or boundary of theirs that made you mad \u2014 after all, they're allowed to say no to you. Does it keep ending the same way, where you bring up something that hurt and then still end up reassuring them they're not a bad person, and the talk never comes back to what happened to you? (The psychologist [Lindsay Gibson](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=VlNpgFWOLPw) wrote the book on this: [*Disentangling from Emotionally Immature People*](https://www.newharbinger.com/9781648481512/disentangling-from-emotionally-immature-people/).) Or can they hear you disagree and look at their own part?\n\nIf it's the first, it can seem like you haven't explained yourself well enough yet. You probably have, and it makes more sense to count on what they keep showing you they can do than on the right words finally getting through to them. Maybe what they can do is text you something kind the next day and not bring any of it up.\n\nYou may not know for certain whether the other person is or could be capable of a healthier response, and neither do they. If they promise to change, that's probably as useful as their prior promises were. Think about how long it has taken you to become more healthy. Could you have done that from one moment to the next based on a promise? Sometimes we can be a better support for someone when we maintain a realistic boundary, so that we can hold high expectations, and still feel prepared for their failure to meet them.\n\nhttps://substack.com/profile/355021294-u-dont-existcom/note/c-349048098\n\nIf [a relationship](https://romance.u-dont-exist.com) keeps making you feel less safe, meditate on why it makes you feel unsafe, and what keeps you in it. Might it be due to a lack of the healed inner child/inner adult relationship? (If so, you're reading the right guide!) But if they've actually hurt or threatened you, even once, old stuff or not, get safe first, which is not a small thing at all, I know.\n\nIf you really did hurt them, repair it. Also easier said than done, but...\n\nYou can mean the apology for a start. Then notice what happens next. Are you sorry for what you did, or are you starting to take back the complaint too because they're still upset? Maybe your first explanation sucked. Try again. \ud83d\ude05 And if it fails again, and you've said what you mean, well, you can't guarantee they'll understand it. Guilt may keep shouting at you. That doesn't tell you the whole conflict was your fault, nor does it prove they're manipulating you.\n\nThen there are people you're just getting to know. Meeting them can turn into a kind of test--\"First impressions are everything,\" as they say, but it could be even worse than that. If the real reason you're asking someone out is to prove you're lovable, or that you're sure what you want, then they've become part of the test. I'm giving you permission now to quit school and flunk the tests. Get curious about who this person is, and who you are, and let both of you have your own reasons for being there in the beautiful present.\n\nIf being alone was a big part of what hurt in the first place, then reaching toward people is part of taking care of your little one too. Do you have to get yourself sorted out first, so you'll be better company? I really don't think so. If the hurt was being alone, waiting just means more of being alone. So I'd call somebody or show up somewhere, mess and all.\n\nAnd reaching out doesn't mean telling strangers everything. Privacy isn't fakery. People can earn more of you by how they handle the smaller things you tell them, and how they take it when you say no. But if a relationship is getting deep and you're still keeping up a fake normal, happy version of yourself to hold onto it, I'd either bring more of the truth in, carefully, or let it be less deep for now. (If telling could put you in real danger, like violence, or being outed where that isn't safe, protect yourself first.) And when someone finally accepts you, it can feel amazing. It still doesn't prove you're meant for each other, or that they've earned your trust yet.\n\nI can't tell you where to look for people who fit you, but I'd bet on more than one place. Keep the good people you've already got. Then maybe try a class, or a spiritual community if one really fits you. Not every one of them has to click (nobody's taking attendance). And if any group, or any one person, starts acting like they should be your only one, that's where I'd want the Protector paying attention.\n\n### Write It. Don't Send It Yet.\n\nIf you've got something burning you up that you wanna say, write it out as a reply. There. That got it out. Ahh. Breathing easier. \ud83d\ude0c But don't send it. Take that breather you earned and then come back to it from a cooler place.\n\nIt doesn't mean you need to hide your upset, or apply for a permit to protest, but choose your battles and execute them wisely, so you don't end up regretting how you communicated later. As Lao Tsu might have said (if I could ever have read that whole boring Art of War book), a war with no casualties is the best possible war. When high IQ & EQ communication hits a writer's block, sometimes the best thing is to simply say, \"I need some time to think about my response.\" And if the conflict really requires a response right now, then obviously just do your best rather than wait for the perfect timing.\n\nAnd yeah, going back to the writer, which is often my inner child when emotions pop up, is part of the process. It's like I'm holding his hand and showing him a better way, not like I just stole his pencil because he wrote something embarrassing. So what was he actually trying to say? A lot of the time it's just \"that hurt.\"\n\nOther times it's \"I did this too.\" My friend Tyler saw a guy smoking in front of a baby and asked him if he thought that was wise. The guy asked if he thought it was wise to mind his own business, and Tyler said, \"Well, I believe in Christ, so I thought you might care about life too.\" Afterward he wasn't sure he'd handled it right, or whether he should have thrown the first stone at all, since he'd smoked in front of a baby himself.\n\nBut Jesus also said to take the log out of your own eye first, and then \"[you will see clearly to take the speck out of your brother's eye](https://www.biblegateway.com/passage/?search=Matthew%207%3A3-5&version=ESV).\" That's exactly what made Tyler the right person to say something. If you actually have the problem they have, and you want to call them out for it, call yourself out for it instead. What we came up with was something like, \"Hey man, when I was younger I smoked in front of an infant, not realizing how selfish that was, and I notice you doing the same thing. I'd ask you to please have some compassion for him. I have a young child now, so this really touches me.\"\n\nIt's also how your voice sounds, though. You can say all the right words, but if the tone is accusatory, it doesn't matter. Tyler's an evangelical Christian who preaches on YouTube, so it meant a lot to me that he liked this take on what Jesus was saying.\n\n### When Healing Turns Into Checking\n\nYou can get hooked on healing too. The wound can be completely real while you're checking it for the hundredth time to see whether you've finally grieved it correctly.\n\nGoing back can still matter, since realizations are a real part of life. But returning to the same wound can also become a search for certainty in an inherently uncertain existence. Spending all day asking, \"Did I process it properly? Did I grieve it enough?\" is clearly not what we're after here.\n\nIf you're feeling guilty because you didn't put your seat belt on, just put it on. If you still want to investigate it later, fine. Perhaps now and then you feel bad about that time you refused to put it on, broke your collar bone in a crash, and then got blamed for not wearing it. That's natural, but doesn't mean there's something new to look at. Then the background checker goes, \u201cBut are you really listening?\u201d There it is. Seat belt's on. \ud83d\ude07 Where were we?\n\nDon't be afraid to ask your therapist/yourself \"What is this practice supposed to help, and how would I know if it's helping?\" This sovereign, gently curious stance should apply to everything in life. Feeling calmer or having deeper insights or explanations for the trauma response are some possible signs of initial progress, but if that's the endgame, it's just a cope. Real healing is ultimately measured by reduced need for the therapy. It's like real parenting: you might start out reading parenting books and thinking hard about what to say or do, but after a while you get into the groove and a lot of it becomes natural. Inner parenting can be like that too. You don't need to keep checking this guide or planning every conversation with your little one; the nurturing, protecting and guiding become more automatic. You might still turn to therapy when you need it, and life will keep offering chances to grow wiser and more compassionate. And if the therapy is actually working, it should improve your daily life, and especially the health of the inner child-inner adult relationship. Going at it harder isn't as useful as going at it with more wisdom and compassion. Take the parts that work, and innovate on the rest.\n\nIf these exercises start feeling like a ritual you have to complete before you're allowed to live, stop there.", "6517490914f1ea44d16497c0201c62fe6d9dfeb3559c20bb7124cf4b63a3a3fd"]];
